1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Explosions of fire, bursts of color and light,

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:09,740
unique artworks, joyous celebrations.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,940
Their language is universal, their appeal timeless.

4
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:18,860
Fireworks, now on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
They make the old feel young again,

6
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:51,180
and the young feel glorious.

7
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:54,660
Even the simplest sparklers demand attention.

8
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:57,120
Aerial spectaculars hold us spellbound

9
00:00:57,121 --> 00:00:59,520
with their power and beauty.

10
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:04,060
To the Japanese, they are hanabe, flowers of fire.

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,900
There's something magical.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,160
It goes back to our basic roots as human beings

13
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:16,700
and the mystery of fire to see the sky explode from darkness

14
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,880
into this bright light and feel these concussions.

15
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:22,380
It gets somewhere right in the center of our brain

16
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:27,120
and says, now this is something special.

17
00:01:27,121 --> 00:01:31,600
An ancient epigram says that he who has smelled the smoke

18
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:34,860
can never again escape it.

19
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,940
And for many enthusiasts, their fascination

20
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:39,740
begins in childhood.

21
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,340
The fun of fireworks when you were a kid,

22
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:50,080
it was the first time you were an alchemist.

23
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:51,940
You could touch something to a fuse,

24
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:53,780
and a rocket would go up in the air.

25
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:56,580
The sense of power almost, you could send a thing soaring

26
00:01:56,581 --> 00:01:59,880
into the heaven or scare the cat or scare your parents

27
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:01,880
in particular.

28
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:04,820
Whatever their age, fireworks craftsmen

29
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:07,620
delight in thrilling crowds.

30
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:11,260
They are constantly striving to create bigger shows,

31
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,600
more dazzling effects, and appreciative audiences.

32
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:20,480
Early each spring, two weeks of festivities

33
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:24,200
precede the most famous horse race in America,

34
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:26,580
the Kentucky Derby.

35
00:02:26,581 --> 00:02:31,580
Half a million fans flock to Louisville, Kentucky,

36
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:36,160
to indulge their love of horses and to experience

37
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:40,120
one of the largest fireworks shows in the world,

38
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:41,860
Thunder Over Louisville.

39
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:50,200
24 hours before showtime, party early arrivals

40
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,880
stake out the best vantage points on the banks

41
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:55,180
of the Ohio River.

42
00:02:55,181 --> 00:02:58,780
30 floors above the ground, in Command Central,

43
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:01,580
show producer Wayne Hettinger oversees

44
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:05,020
the final countdown to Thunder.

45
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:07,720
We're always tasked with kicking off a bigger event

46
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:09,220
for the Kentucky Derby Festival.

47
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:11,460
That's my job, to make sure that it's got a bigger

48
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:12,840
and better look to it.

49
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:15,140
There's no doubt in my mind we're going to lay it

50
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:16,540
on the line good this year.

51
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:21,740
Thunder Over Louisville is the creation of the famed

52
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:25,180
Zambelli family of Newcastle, Pennsylvania.

53
00:03:25,181 --> 00:03:28,780
For the past century, three generations of Zambellis

54
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:31,560
have brightened skies and lifted spirits

55
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:34,260
at events around the world.

56
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:38,760
Presidential inaugurations, royal salutes,

57
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:41,700
the American bicentennial.

58
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:44,840
These and scores of other memorable occasions

59
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:47,340
have felt the Zambelli's special touch.

60
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:52,600
For the national victory celebration

61
00:03:52,601 --> 00:03:56,200
after Operation Desert Storm, they welcomed the troops home

62
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:59,040
with a specially designed yellow ribbon pattern

63
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:00,840
in the skies over Washington.

64
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:07,220
These and other patterns are produced

65
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:10,260
by laying chemical pellets in a shell

66
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:14,960
along a cardboard cutout of the desired shape.

67
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:17,960
Filler material surrounds the chemical pellets,

68
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:20,360
also known as stars.

69
00:04:20,361 --> 00:04:23,260
Once airborne, a fuse ignites the stars,

70
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:25,460
and they burst out to form the pattern.

71
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:34,400
George Zambelli Sr. has directed the company since 1957,

72
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:38,420
and now proudly goes by the nickname of Mr. Fireworks.

73
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,640
For the Thunder extravaganza, he oversees scores

74
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:46,020
of highly skilled pyrotechnicians.

75
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,720
My father would often say it takes longer

76
00:04:48,721 --> 00:04:51,460
It takes longer to be a good pyrotechnician than to be an MD.

77
00:04:51,660 --> 00:04:53,140
So we all know how long that takes.

78
00:04:53,620 --> 00:04:55,640
It just takes time because it's not in a book.

79
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:56,980
You've got to learn the process.

80
00:04:57,200 --> 00:04:58,800
You've got to learn the mixtures, the formulas.

81
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:00,480
You've got to try them out.

82
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:01,660
You've got to experiment with them.

83
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:03,060
It's a long process.

84
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:09,340
George Zambelli, Jr. is an MD, an ophthalmologist,

85
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:12,640
who remains heavily involved in the family business.

86
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,780
There are 24 pyrotechnicians working on the bridge today.

87
00:05:17,781 --> 00:05:20,180
They're not only putting in the final mortars,

88
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:23,420
but they're also checking all the connections,

89
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:25,380
putting in the field modules, which

90
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:28,020
will connect to the main module, and then

91
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:31,220
to the laptop computer.

92
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:35,940
This year's show contains an astonishing 40,000 shells,

93
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:38,440
weighing over 50 tons.

94
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:41,540
Every single shell is handmade, and each one

95
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:44,980
must be carefully mounted in its own mortar.

96
00:05:44,981 --> 00:05:47,720
They are designed with a specific pattern

97
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:53,220
and use chemicals that burn with the prescribed color.

98
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:56,660
Well, we really wouldn't want to lean over one of the tubes

99
00:05:56,760 --> 00:06:00,960
in case static electricity would fire one of the rockets.

100
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:04,040
But standing here, if one of the rockets fired,

101
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:07,540
we'd be relatively safe.

102
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:10,340
Back in the command center, the production team

103
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:13,340
keeps careful watch over the mainframe computer

104
00:06:13,341 --> 00:06:15,840
designed to run the show.

105
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:18,580
It bounces instructions off a satellite

106
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:20,680
and down to the electrical controls

107
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:24,780
tied to each batch of charges.

108
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:27,080
We will be sending signals out to the fireworks

109
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:29,660
on the barges as well as on the bridge.

110
00:06:29,760 --> 00:06:32,960
We're all tied in to the central time clock

111
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:34,720
coming off the satellites.

112
00:06:34,820 --> 00:06:37,260
They can run independently, but the backup system

113
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:39,640
is all coming through here to keep them all interlinked.

114
00:06:39,641 --> 00:06:41,440
I'm going to be playing.

115
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:45,680
Throughout the afternoon and evening, the crowd gathers,

116
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:48,280
and anticipation grows.

117
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:51,680
The main event is preceded by an elaborate air show,

118
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:55,960
featuring everything from war reenactments to UFOs.

119
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:04,160
As the clock ticks down, director Tim Creed

120
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:07,440
is the one man with his finger on the abort button.

121
00:07:07,441 --> 00:07:09,940
Any sign of malfunction or safety breach

122
00:07:09,941 --> 00:07:14,320
can put the show on hold and disappoint an eager audience

123
00:07:14,321 --> 00:07:18,960
that includes millions of television viewers.

124
00:07:18,961 --> 00:07:20,520
Nobody wants a delay.

125
00:07:20,521 --> 00:07:22,820
The time when everybody gets a chance to calm down

126
00:07:22,821 --> 00:07:24,660
is when the fireworks go.

127
00:07:24,661 --> 00:07:28,200
When the fireworks start, it's all on computer,

128
00:07:28,201 --> 00:07:30,300
and we really get to sit back and enjoy it.

129
00:07:30,301 --> 00:07:31,460
It's a lot of fun.

130
00:07:31,461 --> 00:07:34,060
At 930, it's a different situation than 925.

131
00:07:34,061 --> 00:07:41,060
Precisely at 9.30 PM, the sky over Louisville ignites.

132
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:02,760
One of the early effects is the dazzling Zambelli rainbow.

133
00:08:02,761 --> 00:08:04,020
rainbow.

134
00:08:04,021 --> 00:08:06,960
Every color that fireworks technology is currently

135
00:08:06,961 --> 00:08:11,140
capable of creating appears above the Second Street Bridge.

136
00:08:11,141 --> 00:08:15,580
Special iridescent violet, cyan, and tangerine shades

137
00:08:15,581 --> 00:08:18,440
developed in Spain complete the 1 and 1

138
00:08:18,441 --> 00:08:19,840
half mile rainbow.

139
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:31,920
Sticks of aluminum powder running

140
00:08:31,921 --> 00:08:35,260
of the bridge provide a cascading waterfall,

141
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:37,920
longer than any natural one on Earth.

142
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:50,840
By 9.52, the sky has gone dark, and a satiated crowd

143
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:53,540
believes the show is over.

144
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:59,020
But the calm is a false finale, a pause before the grandest

145
00:08:59,021 --> 00:09:02,760
finale the world will see this year.

146
00:09:02,860 --> 00:09:06,060
In the next two minutes, 6,000 shells

147
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:07,660
will explode over Louisville.

148
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:20,680
The show that thrilled Louisville was state of the art,

149
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:23,820
but the technology behind it had come directly

150
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:26,680
from the ancient invention of gunpowder.

151
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:34,020
The computers that ran Thunder over Louisville

152
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:40,060
were connected to over 700 miles of cable and fiber optic

153
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:41,060
lines.

154
00:09:54,580 --> 00:09:58,020
The origins of fireworks date back at least 1,000 years,

155
00:09:58,021 --> 00:10:00,260
and a trace to the Far East.

156
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:04,260
Most historians agree that the Chinese invented black powder,

157
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:06,920
the original gunpowder and the source

158
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:09,260
of pyrotechnic explosions.

159
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:12,500
Legend has it that a Chinese chemist accidentally

160
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,500
combined saltpeter or potassium nitrate

161
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:18,500
with two fire starters, sulfur and charcoal,

162
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:22,720
and thus stumbled onto a combustible mixture.

163
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:25,380
The Chinese made firecrackers out of this

164
00:10:25,480 --> 00:10:26,780
by confining it in tubes.

165
00:10:26,781 --> 00:10:29,480
in tubes, they made rockets that shot up in the air

166
00:10:29,580 --> 00:10:32,080
and produced pyrotechnic effects,

167
00:10:32,180 --> 00:10:34,760
and really created the beginnings of today's fireworks

168
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:37,360
industry.

169
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:42,120
Early fireworks burned in pale shades of white and gold,

170
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:45,900
the natural tints of sulfur, charcoal, and potassium

171
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,160
nitrate.

172
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:49,940
When the powder was packed into closed bamboo tubes,

173
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:53,040
the firecracker was born.

174
00:10:53,041 --> 00:10:56,740
A tube with one open end created the first sky rockets.

175
00:11:01,740 --> 00:11:05,620
The Chinese imbued the mysterious and powerful new force

176
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:08,720
with religious meaning, using its sound

177
00:11:08,820 --> 00:11:11,660
to ward off evil spirits.

178
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:16,860
In time, most significant events, births, weddings, funerals,

179
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:20,160
were marked by the ritual display of fireworks.

180
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:26,640
When Western explorers saw firecrackers and skyrockets,

181
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:30,340
they immediately recognized the military potential.

182
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:32,320
They brought gunpowder back to Europe,

183
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:35,660
where the English monk Roger Bacon cracked the mystery

184
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:38,260
of its composition.

185
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:42,260
Bacon feared the invention and recorded his formula

186
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:45,200
in a secret code.

187
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:48,700
But the Pandora's box was open.

188
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:51,760
And by the 1400s, gunpowder factories

189
00:11:51,761 --> 00:11:55,260
had sprouted up from England to Italy.

190
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:58,900
European nations quickly learned that the new technology

191
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,240
could shift the balance of power.

192
00:12:02,340 --> 00:12:06,440
The real important person in 15th, 16th century

193
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:09,420
military technology was the powder maker,

194
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:12,240
because someone who could produce and knew all the secrets

195
00:12:12,340 --> 00:12:14,820
to make a good black powder, a good gunpowder,

196
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:17,520
determined how far your cannons could fire.

197
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:21,080
And of course, in naval warfare, as soon as your ship

198
00:12:21,081 --> 00:12:24,820
gets within firing range of the enemy ship, you open fire.

199
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:26,780
And if you have an excellent black powder,

200
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,020
it would give you an enhanced firing range

201
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:33,020
and military superiority.

202
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:37,020
Gunpowder and fireworks continued to spread.

203
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:40,240
Marco Polo imported the formula to Italy,

204
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:43,740
making his homeland the fireworks capital of the world

205
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:45,040
for centuries to come.

206
00:12:45,041 --> 00:12:48,380
to come.

207
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:52,380
The Italians crafted fireworks fountains, an effect created

208
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:55,920
when iron filings were added to the mix and sprayed out

209
00:12:56,020 --> 00:12:57,280
in a stream of fire.

210
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:07,120
Royalty across Europe took full advantage of their new toy.

211
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:10,140
In Russia, Peter the Great loved noise

212
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:13,660
and delighted in personally setting off charges.

213
00:13:13,661 --> 00:13:16,560
When his son Alexis was born in 1690,

214
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:19,700
Peter assaulted the ears of Moscow residents

215
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,200
with a deafening five-hour salute.

216
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:30,080
Military victories were invariably celebrated

217
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:32,340
by showers of fire.

218
00:13:32,440 --> 00:13:36,020
In 1749, the end of the Austrian War of Succession

219
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:40,120
was marked by a memorable display of nationalistic pride.

220
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:45,460
There were celebrations all over Europe.

221
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:49,400
And in Paris, they had one where the Italians and the French

222
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:51,800
pirate technicians got into a big argument about who was

223
00:13:51,900 --> 00:13:53,400
going to set off the show.

224
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:54,600
The fight broke out.

225
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:55,800
The fireworks went off.

226
00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:58,000
By mistake, 300 people were wounded.

227
00:13:58,100 --> 00:13:59,600
Some 40 or 50 were killed.

228
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:02,620
All this for the honor of setting off the fireworks.

229
00:14:02,720 --> 00:14:05,220
French and Italians, great tempers,

230
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:06,220
and everything went up.

231
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:07,020
Went up.

232
00:14:07,021 --> 00:14:07,820
Boom.

233
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:13,220
Extravagant structures known as temples

234
00:14:13,221 --> 00:14:18,360
or machines provided a majestic backdrop for displays.

235
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:22,060
These facades could reach hundreds of feet into the sky

236
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:28,340
and acted as grand, albeit temporary, launching pads.

237
00:14:28,440 --> 00:14:30,400
A fireworks show would often involve

238
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:34,080
building an elaborate set, a frame.

239
00:14:34,180 --> 00:14:35,640
And then they would put fountains.

240
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:38,540
They would put wheels that would rise into the air.

241
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:41,720
They would fill this framework with a number of different types

242
00:14:41,721 --> 00:14:42,960
of fireworks devices.

243
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:45,900
It was frequently the case that the set would catch fire

244
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,200
and the grand finale would be the burning of the entire frame

245
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:52,200
as the end of the evening show.

246
00:14:55,140 --> 00:14:57,840
Early in the 1800s, fireworks displays

247
00:14:57,940 --> 00:15:01,700
began to employ innovative set pieces.

248
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:04,480
A wooden framework was built in the shape of a face

249
00:15:04,580 --> 00:15:06,880
or more complex scene.

250
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:09,880
Approximately every four inches a charge was attached

251
00:15:09,881 --> 00:15:12,720
attached to the frame, and a fuse ran the length

252
00:15:12,820 --> 00:15:15,820
of the contraption.

253
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:19,220
When lit, a fiery image appeared, as in this portrait

254
00:15:19,320 --> 00:15:20,920
of President Herbert Hoover.

255
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:28,860
The largest set piece ever built was in England

256
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:34,140
at the Crystal Palace display in 1898.

257
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:36,180
A structure hundreds of feet long

258
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:39,280
depicted a massive collage of quaint houses

259
00:15:39,281 --> 00:15:40,540
and striking buildings.

260
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:47,120
From its Chinese origins up until 1800,

261
00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:50,160
fireworks presentation had advanced,

262
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:54,860
while the underlying science remained largely unchanged.

263
00:15:54,960 --> 00:15:58,260
But with the Industrial Age came modern chemistry

264
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:00,840
and vividly colored minerals.

265
00:16:00,940 --> 00:16:04,340
Strontium compounds burned red, while copper

266
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:05,940
gave off a blue-green flame.

267
00:16:05,941 --> 00:16:08,840
flame.

268
00:16:08,940 --> 00:16:11,880
Americans flock to the exciting new colors,

269
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:15,320
producing a boom in consumer fireworks.

270
00:16:15,420 --> 00:16:18,420
Mass market ads directed primarily at children

271
00:16:18,520 --> 00:16:23,160
became a staple of American magazines and catalogs.

272
00:16:23,260 --> 00:16:26,120
Colorful cartoon-like images entice the young

273
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:27,620
to join in on the fun.

274
00:16:27,621 --> 00:16:32,620
This age of innocence ended abruptly

275
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:37,360
when the United States joined the First World War.

276
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:40,260
The skies went dark on the 4th of July

277
00:16:40,360 --> 00:16:42,160
during the First and Second World Wars,

278
00:16:42,260 --> 00:16:45,540
and the fireworks companies and manufacturers in the US

279
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:48,880
were converted into ordnance producers.

280
00:16:48,980 --> 00:16:51,840
They were a valuable resource for the country to have

281
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:54,380
in that they provided an experience

282
00:16:54,381 --> 00:16:57,580
to have in that they provided an experienced group

283
00:16:57,680 --> 00:17:01,180
of powder mixers and blenders and people

284
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:02,880
who were used to handling explosives.

285
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:06,220
They greatly aided our war effort.

286
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:08,720
Fireworks had traveled the length and breadth

287
00:17:08,820 --> 00:17:12,700
of the globe, migrating from China to Europe

288
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,800
to the United States.

289
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:16,900
The business had transformed itself

290
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,500
to help safeguard the freedoms of the new world.

291
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:22,940
In the years to come, a determination

292
00:17:22,941 --> 00:17:26,280
to celebrate that same freedom would bring fireworks

293
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:28,120
to new heights of brilliance.

294
00:17:31,720 --> 00:17:33,820
The development of fireworks was hastened

295
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:36,580
by ancient alchemists who were attempting

296
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:40,260
to convert common minerals into silver and gold.

297
00:17:40,261 --> 00:17:41,760
gold.

298
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:55,340
In the United States, the custom of marking Independence Day

299
00:17:55,440 --> 00:18:00,580
with fireworks began in 1777, six years before the Revolutionary

300
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:02,280
War ended.

301
00:18:02,380 --> 00:18:04,860
John Adams had asked his compatriots

302
00:18:04,960 --> 00:18:08,880
to solemnize their freedom with bonfires and illuminations

303
00:18:08,881 --> 00:18:11,780
from one end of this continent to the other.

304
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:13,520
And they responded eagerly.

305
00:18:18,620 --> 00:18:20,760
By the late 19th century, fireworks

306
00:18:20,860 --> 00:18:23,820
had become affordable for the common man.

307
00:18:23,920 --> 00:18:26,800
And private citizens from New York to California

308
00:18:26,900 --> 00:18:28,500
staged jarring celebrations.

309
00:18:31,660 --> 00:18:36,740
A backyard fireworks show in this country, say, 1900,

310
00:18:36,741 --> 00:18:40,040
would have been filled with large explosive devices.

311
00:18:40,140 --> 00:18:41,180
There were no regulations.

312
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:44,740
You could go out and buy large quarter sticks of dynamite

313
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:47,560
to make a loud bang, let everybody in the town

314
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:51,160
know that you were celebrating the 4th of July.

315
00:18:51,260 --> 00:18:52,660
From a young boy's point of view,

316
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:55,420
the 4th of July was fireworks paradise.

317
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:59,060
The availability of fireworks was widespread.

318
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:01,740
He could walk into the local dime store,

319
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:05,460
buy whatever his meager pocketbook would afford,

320
00:19:05,461 --> 00:19:09,000
and have the time of his life.

321
00:19:09,100 --> 00:19:12,260
By early in the 20th century, the 4th of July

322
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:15,800
had become a major national event, more important

323
00:19:15,900 --> 00:19:18,400
than before or since.

324
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,880
While most children delighted in their power to make noise,

325
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:25,820
each year a few unlucky revelers were maimed or killed

326
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:28,080
in explosions.

327
00:19:28,180 --> 00:19:30,820
Blank cartridge pistols were popular,

328
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:34,420
despite their tendency to shatter and embed pieces of metal

329
00:19:34,421 --> 00:19:35,320
in young hands.

330
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:43,760
What often happened was that a child would have an accident

331
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:46,260
like this, which by today's standards,

332
00:19:46,360 --> 00:19:50,400
we would consider relatively mild in most cases.

333
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:55,640
But back during those days, the ensuing tetanus infection

334
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:58,380
took a lot of lives.

335
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:01,940
Over the years, approximately 4,000 Americans

336
00:20:01,941 --> 00:20:05,140
celebrating their independence, more than the number

337
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:08,080
of American soldiers who perished in battle

338
00:20:08,180 --> 00:20:10,980
during the Revolutionary War.

339
00:20:11,080 --> 00:20:14,680
The same social forces pressing for prohibition of alcohol

340
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:18,420
embarked on an anti-fireworks crusade.

341
00:20:18,520 --> 00:20:22,420
The calls rose for a safer and saner fourth.

342
00:20:22,520 --> 00:20:26,960
Between 1910 and 1914, several states passed laws

343
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:29,460
restricting the sales and distribution

344
00:20:29,461 --> 00:20:31,020
of certain types of fireworks.

345
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:37,300
While new laws regulated the most dangerous

346
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:40,580
consumer explosives, fireworks continued

347
00:20:40,680 --> 00:20:44,500
to be marketed to children well into the 1940s.

348
00:20:44,600 --> 00:20:49,140
Elaborate catalogs enticed them with low-tech action figures

349
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:51,120
who often ended their brief existence

350
00:20:51,220 --> 00:20:55,480
in a glorious burst of self-destruction.

351
00:20:55,580 --> 00:20:57,580
There was an SOS fire engine, and it would

352
00:20:57,581 --> 00:21:00,280
And it would let off a terrible siren and blow up, which is

353
00:21:00,380 --> 00:21:03,120
very unlikely for a fire engine to do.

354
00:21:03,220 --> 00:21:05,080
There was even an SOS outhouse.

355
00:21:05,180 --> 00:21:07,820
You would see the smoke coming out of the half moon,

356
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:09,020
and a shriek, and then bang.

357
00:21:09,120 --> 00:21:10,220
It would blow up.

358
00:21:10,320 --> 00:21:11,420
It was a marvelous series of things

359
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:15,140
you could find in those catalogs.

360
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:18,340
As consumer fireworks became more limited,

361
00:21:18,440 --> 00:21:20,340
towns and cities developed the custom

362
00:21:20,440 --> 00:21:23,540
of grand communal celebrations.

363
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:26,640
One of the great rituals of small-town America

364
00:21:26,641 --> 00:21:30,180
was young and old gathering in the town square

365
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:34,640
for the annual Independence Day bash.

366
00:21:34,740 --> 00:21:35,780
It was an occasion.

367
00:21:35,880 --> 00:21:38,180
And you learn for the first time that there was pleasure

368
00:21:38,280 --> 00:21:40,120
in a community gathering because you were all there

369
00:21:40,220 --> 00:21:41,120
to see something.

370
00:21:41,220 --> 00:21:42,820
Except it was better than the theater, it was better than

371
00:21:42,920 --> 00:21:43,960
the opera, it was better than the concert,

372
00:21:44,060 --> 00:21:45,400
because it was surprising.

373
00:21:45,500 --> 00:21:46,760
You never knew what was going to happen quite,

374
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:49,360
what you were going to see in the sky.

375
00:21:49,460 --> 00:21:51,840
For young children, the fireworks man

376
00:21:51,940 --> 00:21:55,540
was a figure of great mystery and respect.

377
00:21:55,541 --> 00:22:00,280
He held the power to unite and thrill the entire town.

378
00:22:00,380 --> 00:22:04,820
And like life itself, the pace of fireworks shows

379
00:22:04,920 --> 00:22:08,320
was slower in those days.

380
00:22:08,420 --> 00:22:12,660
A fireworks show 50 years ago would typically last an hour.

381
00:22:12,760 --> 00:22:15,200
Each shell would be fired up in the air.

382
00:22:15,300 --> 00:22:16,500
It would burst.

383
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:18,500
The crowd would ooh and ah.

384
00:22:18,600 --> 00:22:21,800
10 seconds later, another shell would go up.

385
00:22:21,900 --> 00:22:25,540
In 1925, 1930, that was big entertainment.

386
00:22:25,541 --> 00:22:28,840
Many of those Depression-era shows were created

387
00:22:28,940 --> 00:22:32,020
by relatively recent immigrant families.

388
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:35,580
Ever since Marco Polo brought the formula for gunpowder

389
00:22:35,680 --> 00:22:38,080
from China to Italy, the Italians

390
00:22:38,180 --> 00:22:41,060
have been renowned fireworks craftsmen,

391
00:22:41,160 --> 00:22:43,200
searching for a better life.

392
00:22:43,300 --> 00:22:45,660
They came to America and brought with them

393
00:22:45,760 --> 00:22:47,200
their secret recipes.

394
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:52,160
For centuries, the most closely guarded secrets

395
00:22:52,260 --> 00:22:55,300
in the fireworks industry were the formulas

396
00:22:55,301 --> 00:22:58,100
the recipes to make the perfect gold color,

397
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:01,700
the perfect green color, the perfect gold spark effect.

398
00:23:01,800 --> 00:23:04,700
Books with recipes and formulas were handed down

399
00:23:04,800 --> 00:23:09,240
from grandfather to father to son, regarded very, very closely.

400
00:23:09,340 --> 00:23:11,340
You would reveal the family finances

401
00:23:11,440 --> 00:23:15,760
before you'd reveal the family formulas.

402
00:23:15,860 --> 00:23:20,000
In 1870, pyrotechnician Angelo Lanzetta

403
00:23:20,100 --> 00:23:25,300
moved his family from Italy to Long Island, New York.

404
00:23:25,301 --> 00:23:29,200
By the 1920s, his great nephew, Felix Gruzzi,

405
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:32,100
was head of the family.

406
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:34,340
During the Depression, Felix and his brother

407
00:23:34,440 --> 00:23:39,740
made a brief foray to Miami in search of business.

408
00:23:39,840 --> 00:23:42,960
My dad and my uncle thought that the grass would be greener

409
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:45,820
during the Depression time down in Miami.

410
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:49,420
Their first client happened to be a call from Al Capone.

411
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:52,320
At first, my father was, gee, I don't know if we should do this.

412
00:23:52,420 --> 00:23:53,420
This is Al Capone.

413
00:23:53,421 --> 00:23:57,500
and he was afraid, but they were desperate for money,

414
00:23:57,600 --> 00:23:59,900
let's say, and it was their first customer.

415
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:04,460
So he and my uncle decided to do the program.

416
00:24:04,560 --> 00:24:08,440
Local authorities denied Capone a permit for his show,

417
00:24:08,540 --> 00:24:13,240
forcing its cancellation and letting the Gruzzi's off the hook.

418
00:24:13,340 --> 00:24:16,840
The very next day, they awoke to screaming headlines,

419
00:24:16,940 --> 00:24:21,580
announcing the St. Valentine's Day massacre in Chicago.

420
00:24:21,581 --> 00:24:25,780
Capone, it seemed, had planned the fireworks party in Miami

421
00:24:25,880 --> 00:24:29,820
to provide himself with an alibi.

422
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:32,220
The two of them looked at each other and said,

423
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:35,120
oh, my gosh, this is not something we want to be involved

424
00:24:35,220 --> 00:24:36,320
in.

425
00:24:36,420 --> 00:24:38,020
They kind of packed their bags up, left Miami,

426
00:24:38,120 --> 00:24:40,740
came back to New York, came out to Long Island,

427
00:24:40,840 --> 00:24:42,440
and that's where they've been ever since.

428
00:24:44,940 --> 00:24:46,640
For Felix Grucci and his family,

429
00:24:46,740 --> 00:24:48,980
fireworks was a calling.

430
00:24:48,981 --> 00:24:52,820
They worked from dawn until after dark, perfecting their craft

431
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:54,920
and building an enduring business.

432
00:24:57,320 --> 00:24:59,020
When we were small and young kids,

433
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:00,620
we never went on vacations.

434
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:03,720
Our vacations would be going to a firework program,

435
00:25:03,820 --> 00:25:06,200
wherever it may be, with my mother and father.

436
00:25:06,300 --> 00:25:09,940
And it was their way of life.

437
00:25:10,040 --> 00:25:12,040
The Grucci's, of course, from the patriarch,

438
00:25:12,140 --> 00:25:13,680
old Felix down to the very youngest,

439
00:25:13,780 --> 00:25:16,100
have always had this great passion about fireworks.

440
00:25:16,200 --> 00:25:18,180
The one I particularly remember was Jimmy,

441
00:25:18,181 --> 00:25:20,420
who had a system set up in his house

442
00:25:20,520 --> 00:25:22,880
so that when the alarm clock would go off in the morning,

443
00:25:22,980 --> 00:25:25,880
on would come the Tchaikovsky's 1812,

444
00:25:25,980 --> 00:25:27,820
which are in the middle of it when there's a huge firework

445
00:25:27,920 --> 00:25:30,260
burst, so the whole house would reverberate in fireworks

446
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:32,300
and Tchaikovsky.

447
00:25:32,400 --> 00:25:35,660
By the 1970s, the Grucci's had established themselves

448
00:25:35,760 --> 00:25:38,300
as one of the premier fireworks companies

449
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:39,440
in the United States.

450
00:25:42,500 --> 00:25:45,980
Felix, the patriarch, had accomplished much,

451
00:25:45,981 --> 00:25:50,160
but his children urged him to raise his sights even higher.

452
00:25:50,260 --> 00:25:53,560
They wanted the family to enter the world's most prestigious

453
00:25:53,660 --> 00:25:58,460
fireworks competition in Monte Carlo.

454
00:25:58,560 --> 00:26:01,000
It took a long time between my mother and myself,

455
00:26:01,100 --> 00:26:03,960
but we got them convinced, OK, we're going to do this,

456
00:26:04,060 --> 00:26:06,460
but, Dad, we're going to go over there, and we're going to win.

457
00:26:06,560 --> 00:26:07,940
I guarantee you we're going to win.

458
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:09,300
And what if we don't win?

459
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:10,780
We're going to be disgraced.

460
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:13,580
You know, that was a very big thing with Italian fathers

461
00:26:13,581 --> 00:26:18,680
in small businesses anyway, the word disgrace.

462
00:26:18,780 --> 00:26:21,180
In 1979, the Grucci's trekked

463
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:24,460
to Monte Carlo to take on the world.

464
00:26:24,560 --> 00:26:26,520
From the beginning, their effort was dogged

465
00:26:26,620 --> 00:26:29,660
by logistical nightmares.

466
00:26:29,760 --> 00:26:31,660
They brought thousands of shells,

467
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:36,300
none of which fit the local metric-sized mortars.

468
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:39,980
Quickly adapting, they bought every container of Vaseline

469
00:26:40,080 --> 00:26:43,580
they could find and used it to lubricate the mortars.

470
00:26:43,581 --> 00:26:47,080
The master firework maker, Jimmy Grucci

471
00:26:47,180 --> 00:26:51,020
from Belport, Long Island, United States of America.

472
00:26:51,120 --> 00:26:53,220
The entire show had been synchronized

473
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:55,600
to an intricate electrical system.

474
00:26:55,700 --> 00:26:58,000
When some of the fuses failed, the shells

475
00:26:58,100 --> 00:27:02,100
had to be fired manually, destroying the precise timing

476
00:27:02,200 --> 00:27:04,100
that had been planned for months.

477
00:27:04,200 --> 00:27:07,100
OK, Dick, go start shooting.

478
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:09,880
All the problems were forgotten when the Grucci's

479
00:27:09,980 --> 00:27:13,580
unleashed their grand finale.

480
00:27:13,581 --> 00:27:26,320
Two minutes of thunderous noise shook Monte Carlo.

481
00:27:26,420 --> 00:27:29,960
Gamblers ran out from the casinos to see if they

482
00:27:30,060 --> 00:27:30,960
were under attack.

483
00:27:34,140 --> 00:27:37,440
The next day, the competition judge announced the winner.

484
00:27:37,441 --> 00:27:38,680
announced the winner.

485
00:27:38,681 --> 00:27:44,680
... that Spain has received the second prize,

486
00:27:44,681 --> 00:27:48,180
and that Mr. Gucci of the United States

487
00:27:48,181 --> 00:27:50,180
has been awarded the first prize.

488
00:27:50,181 --> 00:27:52,080
Oh!

489
00:27:52,081 --> 00:27:53,760
I had never seen my dad cry.

490
00:27:53,761 --> 00:27:56,020
He actually started to cry.

491
00:27:56,021 --> 00:27:58,960
It still makes me very emotional to see.

492
00:27:58,961 --> 00:28:01,900
I mean, he was in his 70s when this had happened,

493
00:28:01,901 --> 00:28:04,200
and it was such a proud moment for him.

494
00:28:04,201 --> 00:28:06,260
And I saw my brother embrace him,

495
00:28:06,261 --> 00:28:07,420
And it was just great.

496
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:09,900
We will never forget that as long as we live, none of us.

497
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,700
It was a very exciting, proud moment for us.

498
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:19,100
The victory at Monte Carlo pushed both the Grucci's

499
00:28:19,200 --> 00:28:22,300
and American fireworks to the forefront.

500
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:24,320
A century after coming to America,

501
00:28:24,420 --> 00:28:28,280
the Grucci's had surpassed their old friends back in Italy.

502
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:30,720
Just a few years later, they would team

503
00:28:30,820 --> 00:28:34,680
with other immigrant families to create a memorable tribute

504
00:28:34,681 --> 00:28:38,580
to their ancestors and to the land that had welcomed them.

505
00:28:45,220 --> 00:28:48,320
In a 1902 political rally in New York,

506
00:28:48,420 --> 00:28:51,800
a shell exploded in its mortar, causing the surrounding shells

507
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:55,240
to fire into the crowd, killing 15 people.

508
00:29:04,680 --> 00:29:08,880
In the years following their triumph at the Monte Carlo

509
00:29:08,980 --> 00:29:11,480
festival, the Grucci family continued

510
00:29:11,580 --> 00:29:14,120
to fine tune their technique.

511
00:29:14,220 --> 00:29:17,120
Each working day at their factory on Long Island

512
00:29:17,220 --> 00:29:22,060
ended with a lively test of the latest shells.

513
00:29:22,160 --> 00:29:24,160
Our neighbors used to enjoy the fireworks.

514
00:29:24,260 --> 00:29:25,700
They used to come out every night.

515
00:29:25,800 --> 00:29:28,540
They knew when it was 5 o'clock, because the 5 o'clock

516
00:29:28,640 --> 00:29:30,100
would be the blast.

517
00:29:30,200 --> 00:29:31,440
They used to watch it.

518
00:29:31,540 --> 00:29:33,280
It was like a fireworks show to me.

519
00:29:33,281 --> 00:29:34,780
It was like a fireworks show to them.

520
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:40,860
One of the Grucci's most memorable accomplishments

521
00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:46,300
was the centennial commemoration of the Brooklyn Bridge in 1983.

522
00:29:46,400 --> 00:29:48,320
A century earlier, the bridge had opened

523
00:29:48,420 --> 00:29:53,240
to a glorious fireworks display captured by vivid paintings.

524
00:29:53,340 --> 00:29:56,000
The Grucci's were determined to surpass the original.

525
00:30:03,280 --> 00:30:06,520
Traffic on the bridge was slowed and later stopped

526
00:30:06,620 --> 00:30:09,020
as the Gucci team planted its charges.

527
00:30:16,020 --> 00:30:19,100
Then just three hours before showtime,

528
00:30:19,200 --> 00:30:21,700
a fireboat cruised underneath the bridge

529
00:30:21,800 --> 00:30:24,200
and fired its water hoses into the air.

530
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:30,180
We were like, oh my god, this boat's

531
00:30:30,280 --> 00:30:31,300
going to wet everything.

532
00:30:31,400 --> 00:30:33,280
If it gets wet, it's not going to go off.

533
00:30:33,281 --> 00:30:35,080
My mother, she goes on every program.

534
00:30:35,180 --> 00:30:36,780
She wears her rosary beads around her neck.

535
00:30:36,880 --> 00:30:39,420
Boy, she started praying real quick at that point.

536
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:40,880
She must have went through those beads

537
00:30:40,980 --> 00:30:44,820
like there was no tomorrow on it.

538
00:30:44,920 --> 00:30:48,220
Fortunately, only a few charges were ruined,

539
00:30:48,320 --> 00:30:51,140
and the show went off in spectacular fashion.

540
00:31:03,280 --> 00:31:06,080
New Yorkers roared their approval.

541
00:31:06,180 --> 00:31:09,580
For many, the highlight was a Gucci invention,

542
00:31:09,680 --> 00:31:13,860
the bridge and river below meeting in a cascade of fire

543
00:31:13,960 --> 00:31:16,160
they dubbed Niagara Falls.

544
00:31:33,280 --> 00:31:36,280
The Grucci's were but one of the families from Italy

545
00:31:36,380 --> 00:31:39,280
that rose to prominence in American fireworks.

546
00:31:39,380 --> 00:31:43,520
The Zambelli's masterminds behind Thunder over Louisville

547
00:31:43,620 --> 00:31:47,220
began their American journey in 1893.

548
00:31:47,320 --> 00:31:50,500
Antonio Zambelli settled in New Castle, Pennsylvania,

549
00:31:50,600 --> 00:31:53,540
because its climate was similar to the region near Naples,

550
00:31:53,640 --> 00:31:55,540
from which he hailed.

551
00:31:55,640 --> 00:31:58,600
When Antonio passed through Ellis Island

552
00:31:58,700 --> 00:32:00,740
en route to his new home, he was

553
00:32:00,741 --> 00:32:04,440
and route to his new home, he carried his most precious

554
00:32:04,540 --> 00:32:08,980
possession, a little black book with the family fireworks

555
00:32:09,080 --> 00:32:09,780
recipes.

556
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:13,980
They're still written in Italian,

557
00:32:14,080 --> 00:32:15,780
and we have them in a safe that's locked up.

558
00:32:15,880 --> 00:32:18,100
And really, I'm going to have to teach one of my children

559
00:32:18,200 --> 00:32:19,300
how to get to them.

560
00:32:19,400 --> 00:32:21,460
And whenever I need any help, I have an Italian friend

561
00:32:21,560 --> 00:32:22,600
that reads them for me.

562
00:32:22,700 --> 00:32:24,940
And we translate them, and we're

563
00:32:25,040 --> 00:32:28,400
using those same formulas today.

564
00:32:28,500 --> 00:32:30,740
From the early years, all of the time

565
00:32:30,741 --> 00:32:35,240
All the way into the 1960s, much of the Zambelli's business

566
00:32:35,340 --> 00:32:39,380
came from Italian Saints days.

567
00:32:39,480 --> 00:32:41,780
People would march behind the band down to the fireworks

568
00:32:41,880 --> 00:32:44,680
site, and that's how they got the people down to the site,

569
00:32:44,780 --> 00:32:46,280
because it wasn't always right next to where

570
00:32:46,380 --> 00:32:48,000
the festival was being held.

571
00:32:48,100 --> 00:32:51,500
It was down a few blocks, and the band would play the Italian

572
00:32:51,600 --> 00:32:53,000
march all the way down to the field,

573
00:32:53,100 --> 00:32:54,400
and then the fireworks would go off.

574
00:32:57,440 --> 00:32:59,740
Each generation of pyrotechnicians

575
00:32:59,741 --> 00:33:04,520
has passed down its knowledge to its children and grandchildren.

576
00:33:04,620 --> 00:33:08,420
Until very recently, there were no formal studies of the chemistry

577
00:33:08,520 --> 00:33:10,460
involved in fireworks.

578
00:33:10,560 --> 00:33:13,480
You'll learn by doing, starting as early as possible.

579
00:33:16,960 --> 00:33:20,320
When I was 12, I actually got to get out and fire the shows

580
00:33:20,420 --> 00:33:23,460
for the first time and torch off a big 12-inch shell.

581
00:33:23,560 --> 00:33:26,100
So I got to get involved at a very young age,

582
00:33:26,200 --> 00:33:29,100
where now today, with all the licensing procedures,

583
00:33:29,101 --> 00:33:31,940
here in California, you're not even allowed to be on site

584
00:33:32,040 --> 00:33:34,780
until you're 18 years old.

585
00:33:34,880 --> 00:33:37,340
Jim Souza of Pyro Spectaculars

586
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:40,980
runs yet another immigrant family dynasty,

587
00:33:41,080 --> 00:33:43,640
the Souzas of California.

588
00:33:43,740 --> 00:33:46,060
Of Portuguese heritage, the Souzas

589
00:33:46,160 --> 00:33:48,980
have been displaying their unique brand of showmanship

590
00:33:49,080 --> 00:33:51,520
for nearly a century.

591
00:33:51,620 --> 00:33:53,620
In recent decades, they have pioneered

592
00:33:53,720 --> 00:33:57,020
safer forms of fireworks.

593
00:33:57,021 --> 00:33:59,860
In 1969, my father and one of his co-workers

594
00:33:59,960 --> 00:34:01,500
were seriously injured in a display

595
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:04,060
that they were firing off in San Francisco.

596
00:34:04,160 --> 00:34:06,360
And it occurred because of a six-inch shell

597
00:34:06,460 --> 00:34:08,760
detonated in the mortar and got my father.

598
00:34:08,860 --> 00:34:10,560
He'd lost the use of his hands.

599
00:34:10,660 --> 00:34:13,540
And from that moment, after that accident,

600
00:34:13,640 --> 00:34:17,740
the whole family said, we were going to do whatever we could

601
00:34:17,840 --> 00:34:20,980
to pioneer the firing of fireworks electronically.

602
00:34:24,520 --> 00:34:26,220
Since the beginning of fireworks,

603
00:34:26,221 --> 00:34:30,800
blasts had been triggered by manually igniting each shell.

604
00:34:30,900 --> 00:34:34,460
Electronic firing held the promise of putting distance

605
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:38,900
and a measure of safety between technician and explosion.

606
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,600
And by permitting central control of the shells,

607
00:34:41,700 --> 00:34:43,740
electronic ignition also opened up

608
00:34:43,840 --> 00:34:46,480
an entirely new creative vista.

609
00:34:46,580 --> 00:34:49,840
For the first time, hundreds and thousands of blasts

610
00:34:49,940 --> 00:34:53,340
could be planned and synchronized to the second.

611
00:34:56,220 --> 00:35:03,860
By the 1980s, electronic firing had become the standard

612
00:35:03,960 --> 00:35:05,960
for major programs.

613
00:35:06,060 --> 00:35:08,760
The new technology would be put to its most extensive use

614
00:35:08,860 --> 00:35:12,540
at one of the largest displays ever attempted,

615
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:16,480
the 1986 centennial of the Statue of Liberty.

616
00:35:16,580 --> 00:35:18,880
For this once in a lifetime event,

617
00:35:18,980 --> 00:35:26,220
three proud and competitive families join forces.

618
00:35:26,221 --> 00:35:29,720
It was too much for one fireworks company to do.

619
00:35:29,820 --> 00:35:33,260
So the three big fireworks companies in this country,

620
00:35:33,360 --> 00:35:35,800
the Zambellis, the Souzas, and the Grucci's,

621
00:35:35,900 --> 00:35:38,500
came together to work on one project.

622
00:35:38,600 --> 00:35:39,960
People said, this will never work.

623
00:35:40,060 --> 00:35:41,360
This can't be done.

624
00:35:41,460 --> 00:35:42,300
But they came together.

625
00:35:42,400 --> 00:35:43,500
They cooperated.

626
00:35:43,600 --> 00:35:45,880
They joined forces and put on one of the most spectacular

627
00:35:45,980 --> 00:35:48,280
fireworks shows this country has ever seen,

628
00:35:48,380 --> 00:35:50,580
and probably we'll see for a number of years to come.

629
00:35:50,581 --> 00:35:51,480
years to come.

630
00:35:55,080 --> 00:35:57,120
For the Statue of Liberty celebration,

631
00:35:57,220 --> 00:36:01,020
the three families fired shells from 33 different launch

632
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:03,220
sites across New York Harbor.

633
00:36:14,640 --> 00:36:17,000
No single event in history has done

634
00:36:17,100 --> 00:36:20,080
more to expand the popularity of fireworks

635
00:36:20,081 --> 00:36:24,320
than the United States bicentennial in 1976.

636
00:36:24,420 --> 00:36:28,080
Dazzled by shows in New York Harbor and around the country,

637
00:36:28,180 --> 00:36:31,320
consumers began to see fireworks as its own form

638
00:36:31,420 --> 00:36:32,920
of entertainment.

639
00:36:33,020 --> 00:36:34,700
Rock concerts and sporting events

640
00:36:34,800 --> 00:36:36,460
soon incorporated the craze.

641
00:36:50,080 --> 00:36:56,480
Amateur pyrotechnicians staged a resurgence.

642
00:36:56,580 --> 00:36:59,620
Pyrotechnics Guild International, or PGI,

643
00:36:59,720 --> 00:37:02,820
was formed by serious hobbyists as a forum

644
00:37:02,920 --> 00:37:04,920
to share their eccentric passion.

645
00:37:13,540 --> 00:37:15,640
Their annual convention concludes

646
00:37:15,740 --> 00:37:18,140
with the lighting of a superstring,

647
00:37:18,141 --> 00:37:22,380
a lengthy series of explosives worthy of any professional

648
00:37:22,480 --> 00:37:23,380
company.

649
00:37:48,140 --> 00:37:49,040
They're great fun.

650
00:37:49,140 --> 00:37:51,340
They're hugely enthusiastic people.

651
00:37:51,440 --> 00:37:53,380
I went to one where there was a moment of silence

652
00:37:53,480 --> 00:37:55,380
when one of their members had died,

653
00:37:55,480 --> 00:37:59,380
and his ashes were set up in this absolutely spectacular

654
00:37:59,480 --> 00:38:00,880
chrysanthemum shell.

655
00:38:00,980 --> 00:38:04,380
And somebody said, this is a memory of Bill Thomasville.

656
00:38:04,480 --> 00:38:05,900
We all watched this lovely shell.

657
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:08,500
May have made it himself for all I know, and this great best.

658
00:38:08,600 --> 00:38:10,500
And we all remembered him and remember the shells he made

659
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:12,940
and saw very beautiful stuff.

660
00:38:13,040 --> 00:38:14,940
The increased worldwide demand

661
00:38:14,941 --> 00:38:18,540
has triggered a jump in fireworks quality.

662
00:38:18,640 --> 00:38:22,280
Colors are more vivid now than 20 years ago.

663
00:38:22,380 --> 00:38:26,180
Electronic firing has brought precision to performances,

664
00:38:26,280 --> 00:38:29,260
and modern science is finally catching up

665
00:38:29,360 --> 00:38:31,520
with an ancient art.

666
00:38:31,620 --> 00:38:35,160
Fireworks chemistry is a very complex area of chemistry.

667
00:38:35,260 --> 00:38:38,200
You're dealing with very, very fast chemical reactions,

668
00:38:38,300 --> 00:38:39,460
explosions.

669
00:38:39,560 --> 00:38:41,260
You're dealing with the disappearance

670
00:38:41,360 --> 00:38:42,860
of your reaction products.

671
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:44,940
There's nothing left to analyze when they're

672
00:38:44,941 --> 00:38:46,440
and the burst is over.

673
00:38:46,540 --> 00:38:50,680
So fireworks technology for centuries

674
00:38:50,780 --> 00:38:54,580
was much more an art than it was a science.

675
00:38:54,680 --> 00:38:57,160
Chemistry has allowed pyrotechnicians

676
00:38:57,260 --> 00:39:01,060
to understand how their materials work.

677
00:39:01,160 --> 00:39:05,500
Computers have given them unprecedented creative freedom.

678
00:39:05,600 --> 00:39:10,200
A show designer can now chart out a program burst by burst,

679
00:39:10,300 --> 00:39:12,840
changing the sequence with the touch of a button.

680
00:39:14,940 --> 00:39:17,780
In recent years, so-called specialty fireworks

681
00:39:17,880 --> 00:39:20,580
have expanded their repertoire.

682
00:39:20,680 --> 00:39:24,420
At Universal Studios Hollywood, this pyrotechnic show

683
00:39:24,520 --> 00:39:29,760
heralds the opening of the T2 3D attraction.

684
00:39:29,860 --> 00:39:33,400
Theatrical fireworks experts produce a wall of fire

685
00:39:33,500 --> 00:39:38,360
that nearly touches performers, yet remains safe.

686
00:39:38,460 --> 00:39:41,940
Close proximity fireworks contain powders that fire

687
00:39:42,040 --> 00:39:44,440
at a lower than normal temperature

688
00:39:44,441 --> 00:39:48,040
and consist of extremely small pellets that ensure a quick

689
00:39:48,140 --> 00:39:49,740
and safe burn.

690
00:39:49,840 --> 00:39:52,180
And in a break with tradition, their contents

691
00:39:52,280 --> 00:39:53,640
are mixed by machine.

692
00:39:57,480 --> 00:39:59,080
This is a look to the future.

693
00:39:59,180 --> 00:40:02,100
In most firework applications, the manufacturing

694
00:40:02,200 --> 00:40:03,200
is done by hand.

695
00:40:03,300 --> 00:40:05,200
We've developed this state-of-the-art technology,

696
00:40:05,300 --> 00:40:07,000
this piece of machinery that allows

697
00:40:07,100 --> 00:40:10,340
us to build very special and very precise mini-JERBS.

698
00:40:10,440 --> 00:40:13,140
By the use of the computer and electronic scales,

699
00:40:13,141 --> 00:40:16,380
we can control the exact measurement of the powders

700
00:40:16,480 --> 00:40:19,220
so that we can control the burn time and the altitude

701
00:40:19,320 --> 00:40:21,920
for our mini-jurge to be fired very safely on stage.

702
00:40:27,020 --> 00:40:30,020
When the Souzas were called on to provide entertainment

703
00:40:30,120 --> 00:40:32,860
for the California Angels baseball team,

704
00:40:32,960 --> 00:40:35,120
they faced an unusual challenge.

705
00:40:35,220 --> 00:40:38,360
The charges had to be placed inside a water-filled rock

706
00:40:38,460 --> 00:40:39,640
formation.

707
00:40:39,740 --> 00:40:42,340
They devised submarine fireworks that

708
00:40:42,341 --> 00:40:45,980
that can remain dry for hours beneath flowing water,

709
00:40:46,080 --> 00:40:49,520
ready to burst forth when the home team hits a home run.

710
00:40:59,320 --> 00:41:02,220
State-of-the-art facilities like the Sousa factory

711
00:41:02,320 --> 00:41:04,900
separate chemical mixing and handling

712
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:08,300
into many different buildings, thus limiting the threat

713
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:10,760
from any potential explosion.

714
00:41:10,761 --> 00:41:15,440
But sparks and static electricity can still trigger tragedy.

715
00:41:15,540 --> 00:41:19,840
And as recently as 1985, 21 workers died

716
00:41:19,940 --> 00:41:21,740
in an Oklahoma explosion.

717
00:41:27,840 --> 00:41:31,240
Around the world, fireworks are being used in increasingly

718
00:41:31,340 --> 00:41:33,180
varied ways.

719
00:41:33,280 --> 00:41:37,660
Virtually every country has a day of national celebration

720
00:41:37,661 --> 00:41:40,960
when they let loose and fill the sky with fire.

721
00:41:52,700 --> 00:41:57,620
Frenchman Pierre Hubert is a former architect whose trademark

722
00:41:57,621 --> 00:42:00,620
is fireworks in motion.

723
00:42:00,621 --> 00:42:03,420
For a show in Beijing, he fired explosives

724
00:42:03,421 --> 00:42:05,960
from the backs of hundreds of bicyclists.

725
00:42:07,660 --> 00:42:10,660
Bicycles everywhere, everybody has this bicycle.

726
00:42:10,661 --> 00:42:11,660
It's millions.

727
00:42:11,661 --> 00:42:14,660
In Shanghai, it's millions of bicycles.

728
00:42:14,661 --> 00:42:16,660
So it came to my mind immediately,

729
00:42:16,661 --> 00:42:19,660
I have to do something with a bicycle.

730
00:42:33,660 --> 00:42:36,660
Hubert was also commissioned to create a show

731
00:42:36,661 --> 00:42:40,500
for the 1982 meeting of the Western Heads of State

732
00:42:40,600 --> 00:42:43,400
at Versailles.

733
00:42:43,500 --> 00:42:46,100
The head of states are moving.

734
00:42:46,200 --> 00:42:48,400
They are always in car.

735
00:42:48,500 --> 00:42:52,100
They leave the castle, and they go in the park by car.

736
00:42:52,200 --> 00:42:53,620
10 cars.

737
00:42:53,720 --> 00:42:56,320
The president, I looking like that.

738
00:42:56,420 --> 00:42:59,820
So it was a kind of fireworks promenade,

739
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:01,360
always fireworks in motion.

740
00:43:01,460 --> 00:43:06,660
But in this case, it was the public was in motion.

741
00:43:06,661 --> 00:43:16,000
In Japan, Hubert invited an entire town

742
00:43:16,100 --> 00:43:19,980
to join in building a mobile whale, and then

743
00:43:20,080 --> 00:43:25,540
use the beast as a seaborne backdrop to fireworks.

744
00:43:36,660 --> 00:43:48,700
Japan has a tremendous tradition of fireworks,

745
00:43:48,800 --> 00:43:51,300
and they shoot them off on every possible occasion,

746
00:43:51,400 --> 00:43:53,780
spring planting, fireworks everywhere.

747
00:43:53,880 --> 00:43:56,480
And even when you land at an airport in Japan,

748
00:43:56,580 --> 00:43:58,720
you will see usually fireworks somewhere in the distance,

749
00:43:58,820 --> 00:44:00,780
something being celebrated.

750
00:44:00,880 --> 00:44:03,460
Those who rarely have the opportunity

751
00:44:03,461 --> 00:44:08,100
to experience fireworks welcomed them all the more.

752
00:44:08,200 --> 00:44:12,100
In 1982, the Souzas produced the first major show

753
00:44:12,200 --> 00:44:15,900
in Hong Kong in years.

754
00:44:16,000 --> 00:44:18,900
I'm sitting in the control room, not knowing really

755
00:44:19,000 --> 00:44:20,000
what to expect.

756
00:44:20,100 --> 00:44:21,620
The pressure was building.

757
00:44:21,720 --> 00:44:24,120
And then finally, we launched the first 12-inch shell.

758
00:44:24,220 --> 00:44:25,920
It goes up with a rising tail.

759
00:44:26,020 --> 00:44:29,460
And at that very moment, we saw millions of people

760
00:44:29,560 --> 00:44:31,760
with their cameras and flashes going off

761
00:44:31,761 --> 00:44:34,360
and creating this effect I've never seen before

762
00:44:34,460 --> 00:44:35,760
in the Hong Kong harbor.

763
00:44:35,860 --> 00:44:37,760
And at that moment, we knew we were successful.

764
00:44:43,100 --> 00:44:45,900
Those who have devoted their lives to fireworks

765
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:49,520
consider themselves blessed.

766
00:44:49,620 --> 00:44:52,520
Theirs is a craft that inspires wonder

767
00:44:52,620 --> 00:44:56,320
and brings joy to all who behold it.

768
00:44:56,420 --> 00:44:57,260
They love fireworks.

769
00:44:57,360 --> 00:44:58,320
They love to please people.

770
00:44:58,420 --> 00:45:00,220
They're entrepreneurs in the sense

771
00:45:00,221 --> 00:45:02,900
in the sense that they're involved in this lovely science

772
00:45:02,901 --> 00:45:05,680
of beautification, if we were beautifying the skies,

773
00:45:05,681 --> 00:45:09,400
filling the skies with beauty and color.

774
00:45:09,401 --> 00:45:12,460
It's a grand, grand science, biotechnics.

